window.docContents[57].resolve({"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--One-Dimensional-Laplace-Theory":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Harmonic Functions and Model Kernels","header":"5.2.2. One-Dimensional Laplace Theory","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--One-Dimensional-Laplace-Theory"},"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--One-Dimensional-Laplace-Theory--The-one-dimensional-mean-value-model":{"contents":"For ordinary functions, the equation  has no hidden geometry: every\nsolution is affine on each interval contained in its domain.  The interval\nmean-value property then follows by integrating that affine formula around the\ncenter.  This elementary model mirrors the higher-dimensional route from\nharmonicity to mean values while making every analytic step visible.\n\nIf ,  on , and  with ,\nthen there are constants  such thatThis is the complete local classification of one-dimensional harmonic\nfunctions.\n\nThe first derivative has zero derivative on the interior interval and is\ntherefore constant.  Subtracting the corresponding affine function leaves a\nfunction with zero first derivative.  Continuity extends the resulting\nidentity from the open interval to both endpoints.\n\nIf ,  on , and \nwith , thenThis is the one-dimensional ball mean-value identity.\n\nInsert the affine representation into the interval integral.  The odd part\naround the center cancels, while the constant part contributes exactly the\ninterval length times the value at the center.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Elliptic Equations\u0009Harmonic Functions and Model Kernels\u0009One-Dimensional Laplace Theory","header":"5.2.2.2. The one-dimensional mean-value model","id":"/Elliptic-Equations/Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels/#PDEAtlas--Elliptic-Equations--Harmonic-Functions-and-Model-Kernels--One-Dimensional-Laplace-Theory--The-one-dimensional-mean-value-model"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Operational-domain-geometry":{"contents":"First-order extension, trace, chart localization, Morrey transport, and\nRellich compactness use the same\nfinite extension atlas.\nIt supplies the boundary cover, charts, flattening maps,\ncutoffs, Jacobian bounds, derivative bounds, and overlap data consumed by the\nanalytic proofs.\n\nFor a bounded open set with local  graph boundary, the geometric\nconstruction supplies this atlas: local graph flattening gives the charts,\ncompactness gives a finite cover, and a smooth partition assembles their\ncutoffs. We keep the atlas in the analytic interface so that successive\ntheorems can use the same charts and refer to their quantitative bounds.\n\nConcrete instances include:\n\n* the interval , by affine endpoint charts;* every positive-dimensional Euclidean unit ball, by signed caps and explicit\ngraph flattening;* a  atlas for the unit ball, which supplies the inherited \nextension atlas needed by first-order theory.\n\nA supplied extension of any order can also be multiplied by a smooth cutoff\nto obtain support in any prescribed open neighborhood of ,\nwhich gives a new bounded extension of the same order.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.3. Operational domain geometry","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Operational-domain-geometry"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Rellich___Kondrachov-compactness":{"contents":"On an operational bounded  domain, the first-order embedding\n\n\n\nis compact throughout the strict subcritical range formalized in the\ncompactness chapter, including the non-Hilbert endpoint\n.  The proof separates:\n\n1. translation control from weak derivatives;2. tightness from bounded support after extension and cutoff;3. the Fréchet–Kolmogorov compactness criterion;4. interpolation and finite-measure exponent changes;5. restriction back to the original domain.\n\nCompactness is proved in the strict range ; at  the embedding\nis continuous but, classically, not compact.\n\nThe higher-order theory lowers derivative order coordinatewise and gives a\ncompact operator between graph spaces where the exponent and order\nconditions are available.  Evolution compactness of Aubin–Lions type also\nneeds control of time derivatives and belongs to the parabolic theory.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.8. Rellich–Kondrachov compactness","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Rellich___Kondrachov-compactness"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Trace--Endpoint-and-Flat-Traces":{"contents":"The estimate has a short one-dimensional derivation. For a smooth function\n compactly supported on the closed positive half-line,\n\n\n\nApply this inequality to the difference of two smooth approximations:\nconvergence in  forces their endpoint values to converge too.\nThe same estimate shows that the limit does not depend on the approximating\nsequence. This is how an estimate becomes a well-defined boundary operator\non a space whose elements initially have no endpoint values.\n\nOn the positive half-line, completion of endpoint evaluation on globally\nsmooth compactly supported functions gives a continuous linear mapThis one-dimensional model is the normal-slice ingredient in the flat\nhalf-space trace theorem.\n\nAn operator defined by completion is pinned down by its values on the dense\ncore and by the estimate that permitted the extension. Both facts pass to the\ncompleted map without loss: the unit constant on the core is the unit constant\nof the operator.\n\nThe completed half-line trace agrees with literal evaluation at zero on its\nsmooth core and satisfies the unit operator estimate\n\nEndpoint evaluation is first bounded by the  norm on restrictions of\nglobal smooth compactly supported functions. Those restrictions have dense\nrange in the half-line Sobolev space, so the bounded linear map extends\nuniquely by continuity. The extension theorem gives both agreement with\nliteral evaluation on the dense core and the same norm-one estimate on the\ncompletion.\n\nContinuity alone gives ,\nsince test functions vanish at the endpoint. Equality asks for the converse,\nand on the half-line it can be proved directly because a function with zero\nendpoint value can be cut off near the endpoint at controlled  cost.\n\nFor the positive half-line, the completed endpoint trace has exactly the\nclosure-defined zero-trace Sobolev space as its kernel:The half-space and  versions are proved at the end of the chapter\nby a different argument.\n\nEvery compactly supported smooth function on the open half-line vanishes at\nthe endpoint, so continuity of  shows that\n lies in its kernel. Conversely, if an  function has\nzero endpoint trace, approximate it in  by restrictions of globally\nsmooth, compactly supported functions. Subtract each approximant's endpoint\nvalue times a fixed smooth bump with value one at zero. Trace continuity\nmakes these corrections tend to zero in . Each corrected function\nvanishes at zero but need not vanish near it. Multiply it by cutoffs that\nvanish on successively smaller neighborhoods of zero; its linear growth\nnear the endpoint controls the cutoff derivative and gives convergence in\n. Thus each corrected function belongs to , and closedness gives\nthe same conclusion for the original function.\n\nAdding tangential variables changes the target from \nto  but not the mechanism. For a smooth function on the\nhalf-space, each normal slice  is a half-line function, and\nthe endpoint estimate integrated over  is the estimate needed for the\ncompletion.\n\nWriting a point of  as tangential and normal coordinates\n, the library first constructs the flat\ntraceIt is obtained by extending literal evaluation  from the dense class\nof restrictions of globally smooth compactly supported functions. The\ncoordinate split is proved volume preserving, so the one-dimensional endpoint\nestimate integrates to the correct upper-half-space energy estimate.\n\nThe same two facts that characterized the half-line operator are recorded for\nthe flat trace. Agreement on the core now takes place in ,\nso it identifies the trace of a smooth function with the class of its literal\nboundary restriction rather than with a number.\n\nFor every globally smooth compactly supported function, the completed flat\ntrace agrees exactly in  with the classical boundary value\n. Thus the completion is tied to the intended pointwise\noperator on its dense core.\n\nOn the smooth core, boundary restriction is the  class of\n. The slice estimate bounds this linear map by the\nhalf-space  norm. Since smooth restrictions are dense, its continuous\nextension agrees with the original map on that core. This gives the stated\nidentity for the completed trace.\n\nThe model trace satisfies the quantitative estimate\n\nOn the smooth dense core, apply\n to each normal\nslice . Integrate over , use Fubini, and then apply\n. The base and normal-derivative energies are two\ncoordinates of the finite L^2 graph norm, so their sum is bounded by the\nfull  energy. Taking square roots and passing to the completion gives\nthe stated contraction estimate.\n\nWhich boundary functions arise as traces is not answered by the estimate.\nWe first form the quotient of the domain by the trace kernel. It carries an\nintrinsic norm and is realized injectively inside boundary . The\nFourier section identifies\nthis quotient with real , with equivalent norms.\n\nWe use one quotient construction for both flat and curved boundaries. The\nfirst step needs only a continuous linear map; the least-norm extension will\nuse the Hilbert structure of its source.\n\nFor a continuous linear map  between real normed spaces, setWrite  for the class of . The quotient carries its quotient norm,\nand  induces a continuous linear map\n, defined by .\n\nThe map  is injective and satisfies .\nThus quotient classes record exactly the distinct values of .\n\nTwo vectors have the same value under  precisely when their difference\nlies in . They therefore define the same quotient class. The\nformal proof uses Mathlib's quotient map and its kernel identity.\n\nIf  is a real Hilbert space, orthogonal decomposition identifies\n isometrically with . Composing that identification\nwith the inclusion into  defines a continuous linear representative map\n.\n\nFor every ,Moreover,  lies in  and has no larger norm than\nany  with . It is the unique such vector orthogonal\nto . The norm here is the quotient norm; it need not agree with\nthe norm of the boundary value in .\n\nThe kernel is closed because  is continuous. Mathlib's orthogonal\ndecomposition gives the quotient isometry and its inverse law, hence the\nclass identity and norm equality. Applying  gives the value\nidentity. Every representative has norm at least its quotient norm, which\nproves minimality. Uniqueness follows from the same orthogonal decomposition.\n\nThe natural Hilbert boundary space of the flat trace is the quotientIts quotient norm is the least upper-half-space  norm among all\nrepresentatives of the same trace. The induced mapis injective. Consequently the quotient is canonically realized as the range\nof  equipped with its quotient norm. The Fourier\nchapter identifies this range, with equivalent norms, with\n, which justifies the notation.\n\nIn a Hilbert space a closed subspace has an orthogonal complement, so the\nquotient by the kernel has a canonical section. This is the feature that\nseparates  from the finite-exponent construction below, where a right\ninverse has to be built by hand.\n\nHere the minimized quantity is the full squared  norm,\n. Orthogonality to every zero-trace variation\n means\n\n\n\nTesting with compactly supported smooth  gives\n in the weak sense. The zeroth-order term comes from\nincluding  in the quantity being minimized; minimizing only\n would give a different equation.\nOn the half-line, the least-norm extension of the endpoint value  is\n. Indeed,  and\n, attaining the lower bound in the endpoint estimate.\n\nApply the representative map to . This defines\nthe canonical extension . For every class\n, the shared quotient theorem givesHere the middle equality uses the injective  realization of the quotient\nclass. Moreover,for every  with the same boundary value. It is the unique such\nrepresentative orthogonal to .\n\nThe construction is repeated for , following the same path\nfrom the normal variable to the half-space. The one-dimensional input is an\ninequality for compactly supported  functions; only the half-space\noperator is completed.\n\nIf  and  is compactly supported and\n, thenThis is the finite-exponent endpoint estimate used to construct the flat\n trace.\n\nApply the fundamental theorem of calculus to  on the positive\nhalf-line, using compact support to eliminate the endpoint at infinity.\nMathlib's derivative rule for the real norm power expresses the result as an\nintegral of . Young's inequality with conjugate exponents\n and  bounds that integrand by\n.\n\nIntegrating the endpoint inequality over the tangential variables produces the\nfinite-exponent analogue of the flat trace, with the same dense core of\nrestricted smooth functions and the same completion argument. The target is\nnow , and the constant in the inequality determines the\noperator bound.\n\nFor every real , boundary restriction extends to a continuous linear mapThe domain is the completed finite-exponent Sobolev graph space. The\nconstructor takes a proof of 1 ≤ p.\n\nClassical agreement and the operator bound are again the two facts carried\nthrough the completion. The constant  takes the place of the unit\nconstant of the Hilbert case.\n\nThe finite-exponent trace agrees with literal restriction\n on globally smooth compactly supported functions and\nsatisfies\n\nIntegrate the one-dimensional endpoint estimate over tangential variables and\nuse the volume-preserving tangential-normal coordinate split. The base and\nnormal-derivative terms are bounded by coordinates of the finite PiLp\nSobolev graph norm, giving the constant . Restrictions of global\nsmooth compactly supported functions are dense by the half-space extension\nand mollification theorem, so LinearMap.extendOfNorm supplies the completed\noperator, its classical agreement, and the same norm bound.\n\nThe completion defines  through smooth\napproximants of , not through  itself. A second description\naverages  over a thin strip above the boundary:\n\n\n\nEach  is a bounded operator from\n to . It reads  only in the\nopen half-space, so it cannot see values assigned on the boundary, and yet\nthe averages converge to the trace.\n\n\n\nIn the figure the strip shrinks, and the averaged boundary function moves\nonto the restriction  at the rate predicted by the estimate below.\n\nLet ,  and  with\nbase coordinate . ThenFor  the right side tends to zero, so\n in  as\n, along every sequence of positive heights. At\n the theorem gives the error bound only. Both results also hold for\nfunctions with values in a real Banach space.\n\nFor a  function  on ,and Hölder's inequality on the interval turns this into\n. Apply this to\nevery normal slice of a smooth compactly supported function and integrate\nover . Both sides of the resulting inequality are continuous on\n, so density of the smooth restrictions carries\nit to every . The proof is written once for Banach-valued functions,\nand the scalar statement is read off through the identification of\nreal-valued Sobolev functions with -valued ones.\n\nThe kernel-quotient construction does not use the inner product, so it applies\nverbatim at finite exponent. What it yields is a Banach quotient with an\ninjective boundary realization; the orthogonal-complement section of the\nHilbert case has no counterpart here.\n\nThe intrinsic finite-exponent flat trace space is the Banach quotientIts boundary-value map into\n is continuous and injective,\nand its range is exactly the range of . The first\nisomorphism theorem also gives a linear equivalence with that algebraic range.\nFor  the Fourier chapter identifies the quotient with the\nGagliardo space of order .\n\nWithout a canonical section, the existence of a bounded right inverse of the\nquotient map becomes a question about extension operators. The criterion below\nreformulates it in terms of the trace alone.\n\nA bounded linear map\n is a right\ninverse of the quotient map exactly whenfor every intrinsic trace class , where  is its injective\n boundary realization. For , precomposing the mollified\nlifting of the Fourier chapter with the Gagliardo identification gives such\nan .\n\nApply the injective boundary-value map to the proposed quotient identity.\nIts factorization through the original trace converts that identity exactly\ninto the displayed trace equation; injectivity proves the converse.\n\nAt  the two constructions apply to the same functions and differ only in\nhow the exponent is written: the finite-exponent operator is defined\non  with  coerced from a real number, the Hilbert operator\non . The canonical identification induced\nby  carries one operator to the other, and with\nit kernels and quotients.\n\nAt , the finite-exponent and Hilbert constructions are the same after\nthe canonical exponent identifications. IfthenThe map  also identifies the two zero-trace kernels and descends to\na continuous linear equivalencethat commutes with quotient classes and boundary-value realizations.\n\nTransport both source and target along the equality\n. The two bounded operators agree on the dense\nsmooth core, hence agree everywhere. Their commuting identity carries one\nkernel onto the other. The generic kernel-quotient congruence theorem then\ndescends  to the quotient spaces; quotient surjectivity proves the\nboundary-value square.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Trace","header":"4.3.2.1. Endpoint and Flat Traces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Trace--Endpoint-and-Flat-Traces"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Restrictions-of-whole-space-elements":{"contents":"For , restriction of the real physical representative defines a\nbounded mapThe completed restriction space is the Hilbert quotientThus two whole-space elements determine the same class precisely when their\nphysical representatives agree almost everywhere on .  The quotient\nnorm is the infimum of the whole-space norms of all extensions.For representative-level statements, a raw real function\n satisfies MemHsRestriction when there is a\nwhole-space element  such thatThese raw functions form hsRestrictionSubmodule.\n\nBecause  is continuous, its kernel is closed and the quotient is a\nHilbert space in its own right. Orthogonal decomposition then singles out one\nextension of each class, the one orthogonal to the kernel, and this extension\nrealizes the quotient norm.\n\nEvery quotient class  has a unique representative\n.  It satisfieswhere  is the quotient class's \nrealization. The extension has no larger norm than any other whole-space\nextension with the same  value. The induced map from the quotient into \nis injective.  Raw membership is equivalent to almost-everywhere agreement\nwith the  realization of some quotient class.\n\nThe restriction kernel is closed because  is continuous.  Orthogonal\ndecomposition in the real Hilbert space  identifies\nthe kernel quotient with its orthogonal complement.  The orthogonal\nrepresentative preserves the quotient norm and is the unique least-norm\nextension.\n\nShrinking the domain enlarges the kernel: whatever vanishes almost everywhere\non  vanishes on . The identity of\n therefore descends to a map between the two\nquotients, and the minimal-extension theory just established controls its\nnorm.\n\nIf , there is a canonical surjective contractionIt sends the class of a whole-space representative to the class of the same\nrepresentative on the smaller domain.  On  realizations it is the\ncanonical restriction\n.  Restriction to the same domain is the\nidentity, restrictions compose along chains of inclusions, and the\nleast-norm extension of the restricted class has norm at most the norm of the\noriginal class.\n\nVanishing almost everywhere on  implies vanishing almost everywhere\non .  Thus the identity map on whole-space  sends\n into  and descends through the two\nkernel quotients.  The quotient norm decreases because the identity is\ncontractive on representatives.  Every smaller-domain class has a\nwhole-space representative, whose class on  supplies a preimage.\nThe identity and composition laws are checked on these representatives.\n\nAt the level of raw functions the same monotonicity holds, and real Schwartz\nfunctions show that the membership predicate is never vacuous.\n\nRestriction membership is monotone with respect to the domain:Every real-valued Schwartz function supplies a concrete member on every\ndomain.  Membership depends only on the almost-everywhere class for Lebesgue\nmeasure restricted to .  When  is measurable, changing\nvalues outside  does not affect membership.  The ambient submodule\nstill contains raw functions rather than quotient classes.\n\nThe same whole-space witness works on a smaller domain because restricted\nLebesgue measure is monotone.  For a real-valued Schwartz function, use its\ncanonical whole-space  element; its physical representative is the\nSchwartz function's own  class.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.7. Restrictions of whole-space elements","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Restrictions-of-whole-space-elements"}});